            <ArticleTitle>Case of corneal melting associated with the use of topical nepafenac.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1002-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report a case of corneal melting associated with the use of the topical nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory agent Nepafenac.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report and literature review.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A 62-year-old woman with a complicated ocular history developed corneal melting associated with the use of Nepafenac. On discontinuation of Nepafenac, the melt rapidly resolved.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Topical nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory agents have been associated with corneal ulceration and stromal melts. This is, to our knowledge, the first reported case involving the new agent, Nepafenac.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
